Pull the capture sets out of the intersection type of an instance or closure parameter

scala/scala3 #27539 stopped Setup from rewriting `A^{c1} & B^{c2}` to
`(A & B)^{c1 ** c2}`, because the meet of two sets whose elements are known
only later can come out too small. That is not so for the type inferred for
an instance of an anonymous class with several parents, where both sets are
variables Setup has just made for the one object; and without the rewrite
the instance keeps one capture set per parent,
`((Handle^) & (Granting[Int]^)){type Under = fs}`. From 3.10.2-RC1 on such
an instance

  - did not conform to a parameter of the same intersection type with a
    single capture set ("capability any cannot flow into capture set {any}"),
  - was not made pure by `caps.unsafe.unsafeAssumePure`, which strips the
    capture set of its argument's type.

The rewrite is made again for the type of an anonymous class instance (the
ascription Typer gives it, and a definition it is the right-hand side of),
and for the type of a parameter of a closure, which takes an intersection
from the function type expected and failed in the same way. Other inferred
intersections are left as upstream leaves them: the type of a variable bound
by a typed pattern is the scrutinee's meeting the pattern's, and merging
those lets a capability of the scrutinee into a pattern type which is pure.

Found in Soundness's galilei (directory handles), parasite (daemon and task
handles) and aperture (the block passed to `open` and `create`).

